1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,927
You probably remember the sound before anything

2
00:00:02,927 --> 00:00:03,239
else.

3
00:00:03,419 --> 00:00:04,819
Early in the morning,

4
00:00:04,819 --> 00:00:06,286
before the sun was up.

5
00:00:06,466 --> 00:00:08,606
The soft hum of a truck engine

6
00:00:08,606 --> 00:00:09,462
at the curb.

7
00:00:09,642 --> 00:00:11,441
Footsteps on the porch.

8
00:00:11,621 --> 00:00:13,952
And then the gentle clink of glass

9
00:00:13,952 --> 00:00:16,213
bottles being set down in a metal

10
00:00:16,213 --> 00:00:17,995
box beside the front door.

11
00:00:18,175 --> 00:00:19,708
The milkman had come.

12
00:00:20,058 --> 00:00:21,859
For generations of Americans,

13
00:00:21,859 --> 00:00:23,723
the milkman was as much a part

14
00:00:23,723 --> 00:00:25,897
of the neighborhood as the mailman.

15
00:00:26,077 --> 00:00:28,204
He came two or three times a

16
00:00:28,204 --> 00:00:29,723
week, rain or shine,

17
00:00:29,723 --> 00:00:32,077
and he knew every family on his

18
00:00:32,077 --> 00:00:32,532
route.

19
00:00:32,712 --> 00:00:35,073
And then, within a few short years,

20
00:00:35,073 --> 00:00:35,882
he was gone.

21
00:00:36,062 --> 00:00:37,397
Almost completely.

22
00:00:37,577 --> 00:00:40,073
So what happened to the American milkman?

23
00:00:40,423 --> 00:00:41,453
To answer that,

24
00:00:41,453 --> 00:00:42,757
we have to go back.

25
00:00:42,937 --> 00:00:44,686
To the horse-drawn wagons,

26
00:00:44,686 --> 00:00:46,972
to the glass bottles with cream on

27
00:00:46,972 --> 00:00:49,124
top, to the little notes left in

28
00:00:49,124 --> 00:00:50,066
empty bottles.

29
00:00:50,246 --> 00:00:51,451
And then forward,

30
00:00:51,451 --> 00:00:52,799
to the supermarket,

31
00:00:52,799 --> 00:00:55,424
the refrigerator and the plastic jug.

32
00:00:55,604 --> 00:00:57,426
Let's find out what really happened.

33
00:00:57,777 --> 00:00:59,936
In the early twentieth century,

34
00:00:59,936 --> 00:01:02,722
most American homes had no refrigerator.

35
00:01:02,902 --> 00:01:05,866
Families kept food cool in an icebox,

36
00:01:05,866 --> 00:01:08,428
a wooden cabinet with a block of

37
00:01:08,428 --> 00:01:09,630
ice in the top,

38
00:01:09,630 --> 00:01:11,552
delivered by the iceman.

39
00:01:11,732 --> 00:01:14,437
Milk spoiled in a day or two.

40
00:01:14,617 --> 00:01:16,553
So you needed fresh milk often,

41
00:01:16,553 --> 00:01:18,178
and the only way to get it

42
00:01:18,178 --> 00:01:20,052
was to have it brought to your

43
00:01:20,052 --> 00:01:20,364
door.

44
00:01:20,714 --> 00:01:24,243
Dairies in every town ran delivery routes.

45
00:01:24,423 --> 00:01:26,036
Early in the century,

46
00:01:26,036 --> 00:01:28,800
the milk came in horse-drawn wagons.

47
00:01:28,980 --> 00:01:30,983
The horses knew the routes so well

48
00:01:30,983 --> 00:01:33,105
that they walked from house to house

49
00:01:33,105 --> 00:01:33,871
on their own,

50
00:01:33,871 --> 00:01:36,051
stopping at the right gates while the

51
00:01:36,051 --> 00:01:38,349
milkman ran the bottles to the porches.

52
00:01:38,529 --> 00:01:41,006
Some dairies still used horses into the

53
00:01:41,006 --> 00:01:42,848
nineteen forties and fifties.

54
00:01:43,198 --> 00:01:45,300
The milkman started work in the middle

55
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:46,019
of the night.

56
00:01:46,199 --> 00:01:48,592
He loaded his wagon or truck at

57
00:01:48,592 --> 00:01:51,063
the dairy around two or three in

58
00:01:51,063 --> 00:01:53,612
the morning, so the milk would be

59
00:01:53,612 --> 00:01:56,623
on the doorstep before families woke up

60
00:01:56,623 --> 00:01:57,704
for breakfast.

61
00:01:57,884 --> 00:01:58,833
In the summer,

62
00:01:58,833 --> 00:01:59,986
he raced the sun.

63
00:02:00,166 --> 00:02:01,196
In the winter,

64
00:02:01,196 --> 00:02:03,184
he fought the snow and ice.

65
00:02:03,535 --> 00:02:06,762
Milk delivery wasn't just about convenience.

66
00:02:06,942 --> 00:02:08,057
For a long time,

67
00:02:08,057 --> 00:02:09,450
it was about safety.

68
00:02:09,630 --> 00:02:11,273
Milk could carry disease,

69
00:02:11,273 --> 00:02:13,507
and in the late eighteen hundreds,

70
00:02:13,507 --> 00:02:16,268
bad milk sickened and killed many children

71
00:02:16,268 --> 00:02:17,188
in the cities.

72
00:02:17,368 --> 00:02:21,544
Pasteurization, inspections and sealed bottles slowly changed

73
00:02:21,544 --> 00:02:23,872
that, and the local dairy became a

74
00:02:23,872 --> 00:02:27,364
name families trusted with their children's health.

75
00:02:27,715 --> 00:02:29,871
Every town had its own dairy,

76
00:02:29,871 --> 00:02:31,209
sometimes several.

77
00:02:31,389 --> 00:02:33,476
They had names like Sunnyside,

78
00:02:33,476 --> 00:02:35,006
Meadow Gold, Hillside,

79
00:02:35,006 --> 00:02:37,231
or simply the family name of the

80
00:02:37,231 --> 00:02:38,761
farmer who started it.

81
00:02:38,941 --> 00:02:41,651
Their names were printed on the bottles,

82
00:02:41,651 --> 00:02:43,887
the caps, the trucks and the milk

83
00:02:43,887 --> 00:02:44,294
boxes.

84
00:02:44,474 --> 00:02:46,492
People were loyal to their dairy the

85
00:02:46,492 --> 00:02:48,118
way they were loyal to a ball

86
00:02:48,118 --> 00:02:48,398
team.

87
00:02:48,748 --> 00:02:49,769
After the war,

88
00:02:49,769 --> 00:02:51,813
the milkman was at his peak.

89
00:02:51,993 --> 00:02:54,103
The new suburbs were full of young

90
00:02:54,103 --> 00:02:56,028
families with lots of children,

91
00:02:56,028 --> 00:02:58,076
and children drank a lot of milk.

92
00:02:58,256 --> 00:02:59,821
In nineteen sixty-three,

93
00:02:59,821 --> 00:03:02,625
according to the Department of Agriculture,

94
00:03:02,625 --> 00:03:05,363
about thirty percent of the milk Americans

95
00:03:05,363 --> 00:03:08,101
bought was still delivered to their homes.

96
00:03:08,452 --> 00:03:11,209
The milk came in heavy glass bottles,

97
00:03:11,209 --> 00:03:13,296
sealed with a paper cap or a

98
00:03:13,296 --> 00:03:13,966
foil top.

99
00:03:14,146 --> 00:03:16,515
Before homogenized milk took over,

100
00:03:16,515 --> 00:03:18,465
the cream rose to the top of

101
00:03:18,465 --> 00:03:20,555
the bottle and formed a thick,

102
00:03:20,555 --> 00:03:21,461
yellow layer.

103
00:03:21,641 --> 00:03:23,568
Children fought over who got it.

104
00:03:23,748 --> 00:03:25,479
And on cold winter mornings,

105
00:03:25,479 --> 00:03:27,891
the milk sometimes froze and pushed the

106
00:03:27,891 --> 00:03:29,808
cap right up out of the bottle.

107
00:03:30,159 --> 00:03:32,642
Each house had an insulated milk box

108
00:03:32,642 --> 00:03:33,539
on the porch,

109
00:03:33,539 --> 00:03:36,229
often made of galvanized metal with the

110
00:03:36,229 --> 00:03:38,506
dairy's name printed on the side.

111
00:03:38,686 --> 00:03:41,000
Some newer houses even had a little

112
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:43,181
door built into the wall near the

113
00:03:43,181 --> 00:03:45,098
kitchen, called a milk chute,

114
00:03:45,098 --> 00:03:47,610
so the milkman could slide the bottles

115
00:03:47,610 --> 00:03:49,263
inside without coming in.

116
00:03:49,613 --> 00:03:52,318
Families didn't place orders online.

117
00:03:52,498 --> 00:03:54,160
They left a note in an empty

118
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:54,576
bottle.

119
00:03:54,756 --> 00:03:56,437
Two quarts of milk,

120
00:03:56,437 --> 00:03:58,029
one pint of cream,

121
00:03:58,029 --> 00:03:59,179
a dozen eggs.

122
00:03:59,359 --> 00:04:01,613
Or they set a little cardboard dial

123
00:04:01,613 --> 00:04:03,417
on the milk box to show what

124
00:04:03,417 --> 00:04:04,769
they wanted that day.

125
00:04:04,949 --> 00:04:06,747
The milkman read the note,

126
00:04:06,747 --> 00:04:07,923
filled the order,

127
00:04:07,923 --> 00:04:10,274
and took the empty bottles back to

128
00:04:10,274 --> 00:04:12,418
the dairy to be washed and used

129
00:04:12,418 --> 00:04:12,833
again.

130
00:04:13,183 --> 00:04:14,831
And it wasn't only milk.

131
00:04:15,011 --> 00:04:17,597
The milkman also brought butter,

132
00:04:17,597 --> 00:04:19,293
eggs, cottage cheese,

133
00:04:19,293 --> 00:04:21,313
buttermilk, orange juice,

134
00:04:21,313 --> 00:04:22,929
and sometimes bread.

135
00:04:23,109 --> 00:04:24,058
In the summer,

136
00:04:24,058 --> 00:04:26,499
some dairies sold ice cream from the

137
00:04:26,499 --> 00:04:26,906
truck.

138
00:04:27,086 --> 00:04:29,609
A milkman's truck was like a small

139
00:04:29,609 --> 00:04:31,391
grocery store on wheels,

140
00:04:31,391 --> 00:04:33,692
and it came right to your door.

141
00:04:34,042 --> 00:04:35,806
The trucks were special too.

142
00:04:35,986 --> 00:04:38,278
Many milkmen drove standing up,

143
00:04:38,278 --> 00:04:41,013
in a boxy delivery truck with sliding

144
00:04:41,013 --> 00:04:42,492
doors on both sides,

145
00:04:42,492 --> 00:04:44,709
so they could hop out at every

146
00:04:44,709 --> 00:04:46,853
house without opening a door.

147
00:04:47,033 --> 00:04:49,144
Some of those trucks could be driven

148
00:04:49,144 --> 00:04:51,372
from a standing position with a single

149
00:04:51,372 --> 00:04:51,724
lever.

150
00:04:51,904 --> 00:04:54,340
A good milkman could serve a whole

151
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:56,060
street in a few minutes.

152
00:04:56,410 --> 00:04:58,688
A single route might have three or

153
00:04:58,688 --> 00:05:00,229
four hundred customers.

154
00:05:00,409 --> 00:05:02,919
The milkman carried a route book,

155
00:05:02,919 --> 00:05:04,820
with every family's name,

156
00:05:04,820 --> 00:05:08,014
address and usual order written in pencil,

157
00:05:08,014 --> 00:05:10,220
and the amount each one owed.

158
00:05:10,400 --> 00:05:12,594
He memorized most of it anyway.

159
00:05:12,774 --> 00:05:14,503
Ask an old milkman today,

160
00:05:14,503 --> 00:05:16,647
and he can often still tell you

161
00:05:16,647 --> 00:05:18,791
who lived at which house on his

162
00:05:18,791 --> 00:05:19,206
route.

163
00:05:19,556 --> 00:05:22,074
The milkman was more than a delivery

164
00:05:22,074 --> 00:05:22,354
man.

165
00:05:22,534 --> 00:05:24,890
He knew every family on his route.

166
00:05:25,070 --> 00:05:27,204
He knew which houses had a new

167
00:05:27,204 --> 00:05:29,053
baby and needed more milk.

168
00:05:29,233 --> 00:05:31,488
He knew which old widow lived alone,

169
00:05:31,488 --> 00:05:33,869
and if her bottles from yesterday were

170
00:05:33,869 --> 00:05:35,560
still sitting on the porch,

171
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:37,440
he knocked on the door to make

172
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:38,880
sure she was all right.

173
00:05:39,231 --> 00:05:42,210
On Saturdays, the milkman came around to

174
00:05:42,210 --> 00:05:42,806
collect.

175
00:05:42,986 --> 00:05:44,760
Families paid weekly,

176
00:05:44,760 --> 00:05:47,378
in cash, or by leaving money in

177
00:05:47,378 --> 00:05:49,743
an envelope in the milk box.

178
00:05:49,923 --> 00:05:51,285
If times were hard,

179
00:05:51,285 --> 00:05:53,506
many milkmen let a family run a

180
00:05:53,506 --> 00:05:55,368
tab until the next payday.

181
00:05:55,548 --> 00:05:57,194
They trusted their customers,

182
00:05:57,194 --> 00:05:59,066
and their customers trusted them.

183
00:05:59,417 --> 00:06:01,007
Children loved him.

184
00:06:01,187 --> 00:06:03,629
Some milkmen let the kids ride along

185
00:06:03,629 --> 00:06:05,664
in the truck for a few houses,

186
00:06:05,664 --> 00:06:07,767
or handed out chips of ice from

187
00:06:07,767 --> 00:06:09,802
the cooler on hot summer days.

188
00:06:09,982 --> 00:06:11,532
To a five-year-old,

189
00:06:11,532 --> 00:06:14,468
the milkman in his white uniform and

190
00:06:14,468 --> 00:06:16,263
cap was almost a hero.

191
00:06:16,443 --> 00:06:18,585
He came by before anyone else was

192
00:06:18,585 --> 00:06:19,299
even awake.

193
00:06:19,649 --> 00:06:21,042
He wore a uniform.

194
00:06:21,222 --> 00:06:22,622
White or light gray,

195
00:06:22,622 --> 00:06:24,722
with a cap and sometimes a bow

196
00:06:24,722 --> 00:06:27,452
tie, and the dairy's name stitched over

197
00:06:27,452 --> 00:06:28,223
the pocket.

198
00:06:28,403 --> 00:06:31,151
Dairies wanted their milkmen to look clean

199
00:06:31,151 --> 00:06:34,292
and trustworthy, because they were bringing food

200
00:06:34,292 --> 00:06:35,601
into people's homes.

201
00:06:35,781 --> 00:06:36,747
In those days,

202
00:06:36,747 --> 00:06:39,713
a clean white uniform said everything about

203
00:06:39,713 --> 00:06:40,541
the product.

204
00:06:40,891 --> 00:06:42,168
And there were the jokes.

205
00:06:42,348 --> 00:06:45,027
For decades, comedians made jokes about the

206
00:06:45,027 --> 00:06:47,083
milkman and the lonely housewife,

207
00:06:47,083 --> 00:06:49,264
and every child who looked a little

208
00:06:49,264 --> 00:06:52,006
different from his brothers got teased about

209
00:06:52,006 --> 00:06:52,193
it.

210
00:06:52,373 --> 00:06:55,368
The milkmen themselves mostly laughed along.

211
00:06:55,548 --> 00:06:57,957
They had heard every joke a thousand

212
00:06:57,957 --> 00:07:00,633
times, usually from their own customers.

213
00:07:00,984 --> 00:07:02,075
So what happened?

214
00:07:02,255 --> 00:07:05,123
The first big change was the refrigerator.

215
00:07:05,303 --> 00:07:06,831
By the nineteen fifties,

216
00:07:06,831 --> 00:07:09,953
most American homes had an electric refrigerator,

217
00:07:09,953 --> 00:07:12,501
and they were getting bigger every year.

218
00:07:12,681 --> 00:07:15,077
Milk could now stay fresh for a

219
00:07:15,077 --> 00:07:16,082
week or more.

220
00:07:16,262 --> 00:07:18,901
Families no longer needed a delivery every

221
00:07:18,901 --> 00:07:19,780
other morning.

222
00:07:19,960 --> 00:07:21,798
They could buy a week's worth at

223
00:07:21,798 --> 00:07:22,085
once.

224
00:07:22,435 --> 00:07:24,815
The second change was the supermarket.

225
00:07:24,995 --> 00:07:26,116
After the war,

226
00:07:26,116 --> 00:07:29,722
huge new supermarkets opened in every suburb,

227
00:07:29,722 --> 00:07:33,087
with long refrigerated cases full of milk.

228
00:07:33,267 --> 00:07:35,759
And because they bought in such enormous

229
00:07:35,759 --> 00:07:38,564
quantities, they could sell milk more cheaply

230
00:07:38,564 --> 00:07:40,558
than the dairy could deliver it.

231
00:07:40,738 --> 00:07:43,290
Many supermarkets sold milk at a very

232
00:07:43,290 --> 00:07:45,980
low price just to get customers through

233
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:46,601
the door.

234
00:07:46,951 --> 00:07:49,667
Delivered milk usually cost a few cents

235
00:07:49,667 --> 00:07:51,827
more per quart than milk at the

236
00:07:51,827 --> 00:07:52,245
store.

237
00:07:52,425 --> 00:07:54,176
In nineteen fifty-five,

238
00:07:54,176 --> 00:07:56,233
that didn't seem like much.

239
00:07:56,413 --> 00:07:58,370
But for a family with four or

240
00:07:58,370 --> 00:08:01,138
five children going through two gallons a

241
00:08:01,138 --> 00:08:03,163
day, a few cents a quart added

242
00:08:03,163 --> 00:08:04,986
up to real money by the end

243
00:08:04,986 --> 00:08:05,863
of the month.

244
00:08:06,043 --> 00:08:07,460
Mothers did the math.

245
00:08:07,810 --> 00:08:09,667
The third change was the car.

246
00:08:09,847 --> 00:08:11,530
In the old neighborhoods,

247
00:08:11,530 --> 00:08:13,212
many families had no car,

248
00:08:13,212 --> 00:08:15,635
and walking home from the store with

249
00:08:15,635 --> 00:08:17,788
heavy glass bottles was a chore.

250
00:08:17,968 --> 00:08:19,350
But in the suburbs,

251
00:08:19,350 --> 00:08:21,531
almost every family had a car,

252
00:08:21,531 --> 00:08:22,694
and a big trunk.

253
00:08:22,874 --> 00:08:25,635
Mother drove to the supermarket once a

254
00:08:25,635 --> 00:08:28,613
week and brought home everything at once,

255
00:08:28,613 --> 00:08:29,631
milk included.

256
00:08:29,981 --> 00:08:32,140
The fourth change was the container.

257
00:08:32,320 --> 00:08:35,400
Heavy, breakable glass bottles gave way to

258
00:08:35,400 --> 00:08:36,866
waxed paper cartons,

259
00:08:36,866 --> 00:08:39,286
and later to plastic gallon jugs.

260
00:08:39,466 --> 00:08:40,959
They were lighter and cheaper,

261
00:08:40,959 --> 00:08:42,752
and they didn't have to be returned.

262
00:08:42,932 --> 00:08:44,994
The whole system of collecting,

263
00:08:44,994 --> 00:08:48,187
washing and refilling the bottles stopped making

264
00:08:48,187 --> 00:08:48,586
sense.

265
00:08:48,936 --> 00:08:51,077
And the fifth change was the cost

266
00:08:51,077 --> 00:08:52,570
of the milkman himself.

267
00:08:52,750 --> 00:08:53,934
Wages went up.

268
00:08:54,114 --> 00:08:55,507
Gasoline went up.

269
00:08:55,687 --> 00:08:58,400
Delivering a few bottles to each house

270
00:08:58,400 --> 00:09:00,828
on a long suburban route cost more

271
00:09:00,828 --> 00:09:03,541
and more, and customers didn't want to

272
00:09:03,541 --> 00:09:04,255
pay extra.

273
00:09:04,435 --> 00:09:05,252
One by one,

274
00:09:05,252 --> 00:09:08,297
dairies decided that home delivery was no

275
00:09:08,297 --> 00:09:09,485
longer worth it.

276
00:09:09,836 --> 00:09:11,438
Television helped too.

277
00:09:11,618 --> 00:09:15,458
Supermarkets and big national brands advertised on

278
00:09:15,458 --> 00:09:17,225
television every night,

279
00:09:17,225 --> 00:09:20,527
and families started buying the brands they

280
00:09:20,527 --> 00:09:21,602
saw on screen.

281
00:09:21,782 --> 00:09:23,320
The small local dairy,

282
00:09:23,320 --> 00:09:25,767
with its one truck and its friendly

283
00:09:25,767 --> 00:09:28,354
milkman, couldn't afford commercials.

284
00:09:28,534 --> 00:09:31,065
Slowly, the big brands won.

285
00:09:31,415 --> 00:09:33,063
The numbers fell fast.

286
00:09:33,243 --> 00:09:36,509
About thirty percent of milk was delivered

287
00:09:36,509 --> 00:09:38,375
in nineteen sixty-three.

288
00:09:38,555 --> 00:09:40,573
By the mid-nineteen seventies,

289
00:09:40,573 --> 00:09:42,995
the figure had dropped to well under

290
00:09:42,995 --> 00:09:43,803
ten percent.

291
00:09:43,983 --> 00:09:46,797
By the start of the twenty-first century,

292
00:09:46,797 --> 00:09:49,544
home delivery was less than one percent.

293
00:09:49,724 --> 00:09:51,477
In a single generation,

294
00:09:51,477 --> 00:09:53,610
a job that had existed for a

295
00:09:53,610 --> 00:09:55,668
century almost disappeared.

296
00:09:56,018 --> 00:09:58,667
Many milkmen worked their last routes in

297
00:09:58,667 --> 00:10:00,720
the late sixties and seventies.

298
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:03,187
Some took jobs at the dairy plant.

299
00:10:03,367 --> 00:10:05,912
Some drove trucks for the supermarkets that

300
00:10:05,912 --> 00:10:06,978
had replaced them.

301
00:10:07,158 --> 00:10:08,144
Some retired.

302
00:10:08,324 --> 00:10:10,595
And many of their customers remember the

303
00:10:10,595 --> 00:10:12,752
morning they read the little notice in

304
00:10:12,752 --> 00:10:14,626
the milk box saying the route was

305
00:10:14,626 --> 00:10:15,023
ending.

306
00:10:15,374 --> 00:10:18,334
Some milkmen held on longer than others.

307
00:10:18,514 --> 00:10:20,116
In parts of New England,

308
00:10:20,116 --> 00:10:22,252
the Midwest and the countryside,

309
00:10:22,252 --> 00:10:25,055
small dairies kept their routes going into

310
00:10:25,055 --> 00:10:26,790
the eighties and nineties,

311
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:29,660
serving older customers who refused to give

312
00:10:29,660 --> 00:10:30,194
them up.

313
00:10:30,374 --> 00:10:33,728
Those last milkmen became local celebrities,

314
00:10:33,728 --> 00:10:36,319
written up in the newspaper as the

315
00:10:36,319 --> 00:10:37,386
end of an era.

316
00:10:37,737 --> 00:10:40,315
The milk boxes stayed on the porches

317
00:10:40,315 --> 00:10:41,533
for years, empty.

318
00:10:41,713 --> 00:10:44,778
Some families used them for packages or

319
00:10:44,778 --> 00:10:45,800
garden tools.

320
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:48,313
The little milk chutes in the walls

321
00:10:48,313 --> 00:10:50,380
were painted over or sealed up.

322
00:10:50,560 --> 00:10:54,063
Today, people buying old houses sometimes find

323
00:10:54,063 --> 00:10:56,653
one and wonder what that tiny door

324
00:10:56,653 --> 00:10:58,861
could possibly have been for.

325
00:10:59,211 --> 00:11:01,109
When the milkman disappeared,

326
00:11:01,109 --> 00:11:03,007
something else went with him.

327
00:11:03,187 --> 00:11:05,496
The idea that a person from your

328
00:11:05,496 --> 00:11:07,589
town would come to your home,

329
00:11:07,589 --> 00:11:08,671
know your name,

330
00:11:08,671 --> 00:11:11,197
and look after your family in small

331
00:11:11,197 --> 00:11:11,558
ways.

332
00:11:11,738 --> 00:11:13,385
He was part of a whole world

333
00:11:13,385 --> 00:11:15,209
of people who came to the door.

334
00:11:15,389 --> 00:11:17,244
The iceman, the bread man,

335
00:11:17,244 --> 00:11:18,100
the egg man,

336
00:11:18,100 --> 00:11:20,312
the doctor making a house call.

337
00:11:20,662 --> 00:11:23,367
And something about the mornings changed too.

338
00:11:23,547 --> 00:11:25,296
There was a rhythm to the day

339
00:11:25,296 --> 00:11:26,623
when the milkman came.

340
00:11:26,803 --> 00:11:28,843
The clink of the bottles meant the

341
00:11:28,843 --> 00:11:29,683
day had begun.

342
00:11:29,863 --> 00:11:32,137
Children knew it was almost time for

343
00:11:32,137 --> 00:11:32,579
school.

344
00:11:32,759 --> 00:11:34,744
Mothers brought the bottles in,

345
00:11:34,744 --> 00:11:35,961
still cold and wet,

346
00:11:35,961 --> 00:11:38,074
and poured the first glass at the

347
00:11:38,074 --> 00:11:39,098
breakfast table.

348
00:11:39,449 --> 00:11:42,737
Strangely, some of the milkman's ideas are

349
00:11:42,737 --> 00:11:43,129
back.

350
00:11:43,309 --> 00:11:46,600
Today, people order groceries online and have

351
00:11:46,600 --> 00:11:48,208
them left at the door.

352
00:11:48,388 --> 00:11:50,954
A few small dairies have even started

353
00:11:50,954 --> 00:11:53,658
delivering milk in glass bottles again,

354
00:11:53,658 --> 00:11:56,016
for families who want the old way.

355
00:11:56,196 --> 00:11:57,589
But it isn't quite the same.

356
00:11:57,769 --> 00:11:59,613
The man in the white cap knew

357
00:11:59,613 --> 00:12:00,440
who you were.

358
00:12:00,790 --> 00:12:02,612
So tell us in the comments.

359
00:12:02,792 --> 00:12:04,476
Do you remember your milkman?

360
00:12:04,656 --> 00:12:05,830
What was his name,

361
00:12:05,830 --> 00:12:07,918
and what dairy did he drive for?

362
00:12:08,098 --> 00:12:09,515
We read every comment.

363
00:12:09,695 --> 00:12:10,788
And stay with us,

364
00:12:10,788 --> 00:12:13,488
because tomorrow we're going to tackle the

365
00:12:13,488 --> 00:12:16,382
biggest myths about nineteen fifties America.

366
00:12:16,562 --> 00:12:18,312
Thank you for spending this time with

367
00:12:18,312 --> 00:12:18,454
me.
